QID="cc-v112-rec-general-ent-emergencies-tracheostomy-emergency"
COHORT={QID:{
 "concept_id":"v6-general-ent-emergencies-tracheostomy-emergency",
 "canonical_topic":"Tracheostomy Emergency",
 "prompt":"An adult with a tracheostomy suddenly becomes distressed and hypoxemic with poor tidal volumes and rising airway pressures. You do not yet know whether the tube is obstructed, partially displaced, or in a false passage, and the bedside team is unsure whether the patient has a tracheostomy or a total-laryngectomy stoma. Walk through the resident's immediate rescue sequence, including oxygenation routes, inner-cannula and suction-catheter checks, cuff management, when to remove the tube, how fresh versus mature stoma age changes reinsertion, and when oral versus stomal ventilation is appropriate?",
 "answer_text":"Foundation — treat this first as an oxygenation emergency, not as a tube-replacement puzzle. Call for airway/ENT help, bring difficult-airway and tracheostomy equipment, attach standard monitoring and use waveform capnography as early as practical. Immediately establish what operation created the neck airway. A conventional tracheostomy usually leaves the upper airway in continuity with the lungs, although the original disease may make that route unusable. A total laryngectomy permanently disconnects the mouth and nose from the trachea; oral or nasal oxygenation and intubation cannot ventilate the lungs. If the anatomy is uncertain, apply high-flow oxygen to both the face and the stoma while the distinction is clarified.\n\nFirst reversible causes — remove anything that can externally occlude flow: a speaking valve, cap, heat-moisture device or obstructed circuit. Remove the inner cannula if present because a mucus-plugged inner cannula can create complete obstruction. Then pass a suction catheter gently through the tube. A catheter that passes freely into the trachea supports tube patency and allows suction of secretions; inability to pass beyond the tube tip is a major red flag for obstruction, kinking or displacement. Do not repeatedly force a catheter against resistance.\n\nThe false-passage danger — do not respond to uncertain tube position by repeatedly squeezing the bag harder. If there is absent waveform ETCO2, unexpectedly high pressure, poor chest rise, rapidly developing neck/chest subcutaneous emphysema or a suction catheter cannot pass, assume displacement until proven otherwise. Positive-pressure ventilation through a tube lying in pretracheal soft tissue can expand a false passage, cause pneumomediastinum or pneumothorax, and distort the neck enough to make subsequent oral or stomal rescue harder. Bronchoscopy through the tube is useful when immediately available and the patient can tolerate the seconds required, but oxygenation must not be delayed for diagnostic perfection.\n\nCuff step — if the tube has a cuff, deflate it while reassessing airflow and oxygenation. In a patient whose native upper airway remains patent, cuff deflation may permit gas to pass around a partially displaced or obstructed tube and through the larynx. It also reduces the chance that an inflated cuff is anchoring a malpositioned tube in soft tissue. Cuff deflation is not itself proof that the airway is safe; it is one step in restoring a usable gas pathway.\n\nWhen the tube is the problem — modern NTSP guidance differs from the older instinct to leave every tracheostomy tube in place until a replacement is ready. Once blockage or displacement is established and the tube is preventing oxygenation, remove the ineffective tube early rather than treating removal as a last resort. Continue oxygen to the stoma and, for a tracheostomy patient whose upper airway can conduct gas, to the face. A simple mask or supraglottic interface can be used over the stoma for rescue oxygenation/ventilation, while face-mask ventilation can be attempted through the native airway with the stoma occluded when appropriate. In a total-laryngectomy patient, ventilation is through the stoma only.\n\nFresh versus mature tract — this distinction changes the reinsertion risk. In a fresh or immature tracheostomy, the tract can collapse or separate from the anterior tracheal wall; blind reinsertion may create a false passage. Repeated probing is particularly dangerous. If the patient has a usable upper airway, oral intubation is often the safer rescue while ENT/anesthesia prepares controlled stomal replacement. If the upper airway is not usable, expert stomal rescue may require direct visualization, bronchoscopy, a bougie or another controlled technique based on the surgical anatomy. In a mature, well-formed tract, cautious replacement of the same-size or one-size-smaller tracheostomy tube may be reasonable, but it should advance easily; resistance is a stop sign, not an invitation to force. Confirm intratracheal position with capnography, chest rise and, when needed, bronchoscopy before committing to positive pressure.\n\nRoute selection is anatomy, not habit — ask why the patient needed the tracheostomy. Someone with a patent larynx who had a tracheostomy for prolonged ventilation may be readily intubated orally after tube loss. Someone with critical upper-airway obstruction may technically have a tracheostomy rather than a laryngectomy but still be impossible to ventilate from above. The bedhead sign, operative history, prior airway note and stoma maturity therefore matter in the first minute. The resident should never say simply 'trach patient, bag the mouth' or 'neck stoma, bag the stoma' without defining the airway continuity.\n\nSenior rescue — if oxygenation is failing despite tube removal and simple face/stoma ventilation, escalate early to the route most likely to succeed: oral intubation when there is upper-airway continuity and access, controlled stomal intubation when the tract/anatomy is suitable, or surgical revision when neither route is reliably usable. Use fiberoptic visualization as an aid to placement rather than repeatedly instrumenting a deteriorating airway. Treat secondary complications created by the event—pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, aspiration or hypoxic arrest—in parallel once an oxygenation route is being restored.\n\nBleeding is a different emergency phenotype — brisk tracheostomy hemorrhage or even a sentinel bleed in the appropriate postoperative/time-course setting raises concern for tracheo-innominate fistula and should not be managed as a simple obstruction/dislodgement problem. Activate massive hemorrhage and airway help, use temporizing cuff/digital compression maneuvers when indicated, and move urgently toward definitive operative/endovascular control. The repository's separate TIF rescue pathway remains the detailed choreography; the key here is recognizing that blood at a tracheostomy can signal a lethal vascular problem.\n\nTextbook-versus-current distinction — the durable textbook teaching remains correct: know the tracheostomy anatomy, determine whether the tract is mature, respect false-passage risk, and understand whether the native upper airway is available. Contemporary NTSP guidance sharpens the sequence under stress: oxygenation comes before tube preservation, oxygen goes to both potential airways when anatomy is uncertain, waveform capnography is used early, the inner cannula comes out before suction, ventilation through a potentially displaced tube is avoided, and an established blocked/displaced tube is removed promptly rather than as a final maneuver.\n\nSenior synthesis — think OXYGENATE, IDENTIFY, OPEN, PASS, DEFLATE, REMOVE, ROUTE, REPLACE. OXYGENATE the patient and call help; IDENTIFY tracheostomy versus laryngectomy and upper-airway patency; OPEN the circuit by removing cap/valve and inner cannula; PASS a suction catheter without force; DEFLATE the cuff and reassess; REMOVE a proven ineffective tube; choose the correct ROUTE for face versus stoma ventilation; and REPLACE only with a strategy appropriate to stoma maturity and anatomy. The dangerous errors are bagging harder through a false passage, blindly probing a fresh tract, attempting oral ventilation in a laryngectomy, preserving a blocked tube while the patient desaturates, and forgetting that bleeding may represent TIF rather than routine tube trauma.",
 "explanation":"A tracheostomy emergency is an oxygenation-and-anatomy problem before it is a tube-exchange problem. The safe resident distinguishes tracheostomy from laryngectomy, rapidly removes simple obstructions, uses a suction catheter and capnography to detect loss of patency, avoids positive pressure through a suspected false passage, removes an established blocked/displaced tube, and chooses oral versus stomal rescue according to airway continuity and tract maturity.",
 "board_pearl":"In a crashing neck-breather, oxygenate first; if anatomy is uncertain give oxygen to face and stoma, remove the inner cannula before suction, never force positive pressure through a suspected displaced tube, and remember that a fresh tracheostomy is a false-passage trap while a laryngectomy can only be ventilated through the stoma.",
 "depth_layers_v206":{"foundation":"Distinguish tracheostomy from total laryngectomy, understand upper-airway continuity, tube/inner-cannula obstruction, cuff function, stoma maturity and false-passage anatomy.","application":"Prioritize oxygenation, remove external and inner-cannula obstruction, pass a suction catheter, use waveform capnography, deflate the cuff, remove an established blocked/displaced tube and select face versus stoma rescue ventilation based on anatomy.","senior_decision":"Avoid positive pressure through a suspected false passage, choose oral versus controlled stomal intubation according to native-airway patency and fresh-versus-mature tract, stop repeated traumatic reinsertion, recognize TIF-pattern bleeding and escalate to definitive airway/vascular rescue."},
 "common_traps_v206":[
  "Assuming every neck stoma is a conventional tracheostomy and attempting oral ventilation in a total-laryngectomy patient. The mouth and nose are not connected to the lungs after total laryngectomy.",
  "Bagging more forcefully through a tube with absent ETCO2, high resistance and no chest rise. If the tube is pretracheal, positive pressure can create massive surgical emphysema, pneumomediastinum and pneumothorax.",
  "Starting with deep suction while an obstructed inner cannula is still in place. Remove the speaking valve/cap and inner cannula first so a simple removable obstruction is not missed.",
  "Repeatedly forcing a tracheostomy tube or obturator into a fresh tract after decannulation. An immature tract can separate from the trachea, and blind probing preferentially creates a false passage.",
  "Protecting the existing tube at all costs while the patient becomes progressively hypoxemic. Once obstruction or displacement is established, an ineffective tube should be removed so alternative oxygenation routes can work.",
  "Treating cuff deflation as an optional afterthought. Deflation may restore a pathway around a partially displaced tube in patients with a patent native airway and may free a malpositioned cuff from soft tissue.",
  "Choosing oral versus stomal rescue based only on the word tracheostomy. The original indication may make the upper airway unusable even though an anatomic connection still exists.",
  "Assuming any blood around a tracheostomy is minor suction trauma. A sentinel or brisk bleed can herald tracheo-innominate fistula and requires immediate hemorrhage/airway escalation rather than routine tube troubleshooting."
 ],
 "deliberate_review_v206":"Selected from the exact successful v20.5 live-canonical backlog as the highest-priority candidate (priority 14; existing answer approximately 70 words). It outranked lower-value lexical candidates because failure to distinguish obstruction from displacement, tracheostomy from laryngectomy, and fresh from mature stoma can cause immediate hypoxic injury, false-passage pressurization, failed rescue or death.",
 "source_refs_v206":SOURCE_REFS_V206,
}}

